  "live_records": {
    "q-122319": {
      "dom_id": "step-q122319",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14122526/assets/b23ef6e4df05874a715271c578dfa57d5ddacabef2d0801edc4e736c7de66e17.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
