    "q-122349": {
      "dom_id": "step-q122349",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=1\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14122526/assets/141c6dd6c91841238298b86fac1063c76faeb7cd103e13d2723f6ac05ed58f50.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(0+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+2)^{3} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+2)^{3} & =12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=8,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{3}=12t+8.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3} & =12t+8 \\\\ x+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
