    "q-122352": {
      "dom_id": "step-q122352",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=1\\,m$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14122526/assets/f08c9d1e8dce3c0ae7c37719cadc3fc60bf5de3030ce8c44ca7528a98f3abf7e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -8{e}^{1-x}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8{e}^{0}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16{e}^{1-x} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4{e}^{(1-x)/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{(1-x)/2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ 2{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=1$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{(x-1)/2}=2t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
